course: dictionary
lesson: 8350
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning you need. The word rope is a good example. Its context is the sentence it sits in. «The sailors used a rope to tie up the boat.» Here rope is the strong, thick cord used for tying.
explain: Look at the job the word does in the sentence. «Dad ropes the calf to the fence.» Ropes is an action word here, so rope means to tie with a rope, or to catch an animal with a loop of rope. Ask yourself: is this word a thing, or something someone does?
explain: Some phrases use rope in a special way, so read the whole sentence. «Sam is learning the ropes at his new job» means he is learning how to do it. «The team was on the ropes after three losses» means they were in trouble. The phrase has its own meaning, which is not about real rope at all.
fact: A rope is a strong, thick cord made of twisted strands.
fact: A skipping rope, or jump rope in the USA, is a rope you jump over.
fact: «Learn the ropes» means learn how to do a job.
fact: «On the ropes» means in trouble.
fact: Twisting many thin strands together makes a rope much stronger than one thin strand.
exercise: «The horse walked along the tow-path, pulling the barge with a long rope.» What is the rope here? | *A strong, thick cord used for pulling | A skipping rope you jump over | A boat pushed along with a long pole | To tie with a rope
exercise: «Ben wants to rope the calf at the farm.» What does rope mean here? | To learn how to do a job | To be in trouble | *To catch an animal with a loop of rope | A rope you jump over
exercise: «Sam is learning the ropes at his new job.» What does learn the ropes mean? | *Learn how to do the job | Stop working for the day | Be in trouble with the boss | Tie the job up with a rope
test: What is a skipping rope? | *A rope you jump over | Strong rope for climbing up high | Rope for tying a boat to the shore | A cord used for pulling a barge along a canal
test: «The team was on the ropes after losing three games.» What does on the ropes mean? | Winning easily | Learning a new game | Tied up with rope | *In trouble
exam: Which sentence uses rope as a verb? | «Pass the rope to me.» | «The farmer bought a long rope.» | «The rope was thick and strong.» | *«The farmer ropes the calf to the post.»
